      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear, presenting that emotional state as true. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not remove the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and the duties attached to it rather than making a new promise. She presents herself as returning the love, obedience, and honour owed by a daughter; Lear does not accept this formulation as an adequate response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"How, how, Cordelia? Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The target is a controlled, propositionally explicit answer, not a reflex-like discharge of affect. Cordelia expressly describes difficulty turning her inward feeling into verbal display, and she carefully qualifies the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the utterance functions as a restrained refusal to participate in Lear's demand for competitive, expansive declarations. Its limiting qualification establishes a boundary against exaggeration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear asks his daughters to state how much they love him while linking their answers to divisions of his bounty. After her sisters give expansive declarations, Cordelia answers Lear directly with a deliberately bounded avowal of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia does not clearly undertake a new future obligation. She invokes an already existing parent-child bond and affirms the fitting duties of love, obedience, and honour that she returns to Lear. Lear rejects the answer as inadequate and asks her to revise it, so there is no affirmative uptake of her formulation.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the avowal and the invocation of filial duty. It does not independently settle every question about sincerity, future conduct, or the full history of the relationship; no omitted context is treated as absent from the work.\"}"
        }
      ],
